A||N||18||Seanh||Engine Bay cooling options?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||05-09-2001||10:59 PM||||I am definatly wanting to cool down the engine bay in my 86GT, it really gets hot Iv noticed. <P>First thing I'm doing is installing a couple of dash switchs. One will control the radiator fan so I can turn it on whenever I feel like it, and when swiched off it will come on when the sensor closes. Second one will control the engine bay cooling fan. I want this to be the same setup as with above, comes on when I turn it on, or when coolant sensor closes.<P>I was looking at the engine vents/grills and have to think that the bottem metal screen is fairly restrictive to air flow. Can I just drill out the rivits and remove it? Perhaps replace it with some mesh screen or somthing if I even need to replace it, im not too worried about fires or leaves.<P>Do they make a more powerful fan to replace the stock engine bay cooling fan? If so where can I get it and how much?<P>I do, in the future, plan on doing to a reward facing scoop, but don't have the money, nor time right now.<P>I read in the "Fiero Cave" that people have installed fans under the engine bay cooling vents. What fans to use? Where to get them? Are they waterproof/all weather? Any problems with this? <P>I would love to put the vents on the front of the car, that go down into the hood. Like I said before, I don't have the money or time to do so now so that will have to probally wait till next year/this comming winter. <P>Any other ideas on what can be done to keep the bay cooler?<P>Also, does anyone know/have a dash mounted guage system that monitors engine bay temp? I would like to do this, and mount a digital guage on my dash. I do know electronics, but Iv never done somthing like that before so let me know any ideas about that?<P>The reason I'm wanting to do this is obvoisly for a cool engine when racing. A friend of mine ran 2 races, 1st one he had been driving for a while, and didn't let the car cool down much before racing. Second time he covered his intake manifold in bags of ice and let it cool it down for a couple minuts and did a heck of alot better with the iced manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spektyr||05-10-2001||06:09 AM||||The switches aren't a bad idea, I may do this at some point, but I've got other gremlins I'm chasing at the moment.<BR>As for the screens under the deck vents, I took mine off, you just need to keep an eye on how much leaves and stuff get down in there.  I'm also going to be building a rain guard for the battery, at least until I get around to moving it to the front of the car.  I'm not sure exactly how much difference having the screens out makes temperature-wise, but it certainly felt like more heat was coming out with them removed.<P>Not sure on the fans, that's something else I've been thinking of adding.  The best heat-reducer I've found is using lower temp thermo and fan switches.  I'm running a 160 thermo and 176 fan switch and the car does NOT get hot now.  Not even close.  However I'm running rather rich at the moment, and that's probably due to my tinkering.  If I had it to do over, I'd probably go with the 180 thermo and whatever fan switch goes with it.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cozmo||05-10-2001||08:23 AM||||All your plans make real good sense!  <BR>Ogre's writeups on this subject are excellent.  Any reduction on average engine bay operating temp. will make the car run better and extend the life of all those expensive and hard to change parts <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> in there...<BR>Don't know if I'd mess with lower temp. thermostats, the "system" likes to run best at it's original design temps.<BR>   The front/rear scoops aren't that expensive to integrate and may provide the most improvement.<BR>   I saw some fans at Autozone (I think) that were available in 10", 12" and 14" dia. with an adjustable thermostat for under $80. One of them may work for the vent areas. Otherwise, measure the size range that would fit and go on an expedition to your local boneyard and look for a suitable type.<BR>Living in Florida and liking A/C, this topic is really "hot" with me... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR><B>C02M0</B><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||terryk||05-10-2001||11:25 AM||||I was looking at some Geo Metro cooling fans. They look like they'd fit on the bottom of the grills and could be controlled (via a relay) by the coolant fan switch or ECM depending on your application.<P>Heat? You want heat? Try leaning over the vents when a 3800 Supercharged is in there after some long runs with boost.  Christ almighty!<P>I want to suck some heat out of there too. Maybe I'll grab a couple of fans this weekend.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-10-2001||01:19 PM||||RV stores sell a 12 volt fan that can be mounted in a 4 inch diameter duct. They use them to circulate furnace heat in motor homes and travel trailers. They move a ton of air and are very small, maybe 5 inches around and 4 or 5 inches thick, you could just tap it into the OEM rear cooling fan for power, and find a good spot to mount it with some appropriate ducts exiting in the fender wells or something.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||05-10-2001||01:20 PM||||Here is my cooling Mod  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1249362&a=12780538&p=47700498&Sequence=0&res=high"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1249362&a=12780538&p=47700497&Sequence=0&res=high"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000006||Mickey_Moose||05-10-2001||05:52 PM||||You could always try wrapping the exhaust pipes in heat cloth - you know the same stuff that they use on the headers on sprint cars. Suppose to keep the exhaust a bit cooler||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||05-10-2001||06:54 PM||||I was thinking in school today that putting fans under the vents might not help as much as I thought it would. I would think that more air could escape just going down the road and comming out the vents. Fans under there may not be all that great of an idea, maybe good for long periods of idleing, but I don't do that very much.<P>I have also removed my battery heat shield. I was thinking about it, and think that is a pretty good idea. Iv had it off for over a month with no problems. I would think that the increased air flow over the battery would actually keep it and the engine bay cooler then with having a restrictive peice of plastic there that will keep heat off the battery but not let the air circulate around it. Any Input?<P>Hey, would my local Advance Auto Parts stock a 176 deg. fan switch? I might just have to buy one of those, but theres not way I would use a lower temp thermostat. My engine itself runs cool as it is now, but its more the intake manifold temp (and engine bay in general) that I want to keep down. How much do those 176 fan switches cost? I am going to put one on if there not all as high priced as the fiero store is, if so I can stick with my switch idea. Anyone know the best place to tap into the wires to manually turn the fan on? Also, where would I tap into the wires so that I can operate the engine bay fan, and raditor fans seperatly? See, I want both to turn on automatically when the fan switchs closes, but I want to be able to turn each one on independantly of each other soon then the fan switch if I desire. Escentially, I want to run the engine bay cooling fan pretty much all the time, and the rad. fan only when racing otherwise on at 176 all the time. See what I mean? Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-10-2001||07:10 PM||||One of the pages in my heat management secion tells you how to install a second thermal switch for the V6 aux fan. You would have to use that circuit to use a manual switch as well. (Just use a togle in place of the sensor.)<P>The grate fans are to aid cooling at lower speed where there often is little or no air flow thru the bay.<P>RV duct fans would likely work but probably would not last. They aren't made for weather exposure.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||05-10-2001||07:14 PM||||If I do indeed go the route listed on your page, would the bay cooling fan still come on when the fan switch comes on? I would like it to come on when I either flip the switch or the temp sensor closes. Because I could easily forget about turning it on, but then again, I'll probally leave it on all the time anyway. That wouldn't hurt would it? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dave Gunsul||05-10-2001||10:17 PM||||The Fiero store sells a Jet Hot coated crossover pipe that will help cool the bay.<BR>You can also Jet Hot your manifolds or wrap them, install side scoops and make them functional, install a deck lid scoop like<BR>Josh has above (very nice BTW, if I hadn't had mine painted last year I would have done this.), use a 10w30 oil in the summer switch back to 5w30 in the winter, you can also cut<BR>out the screens on the bottom of the deck lid vents to let more air out. If you do this<BR>you can drill the holes out and use small screws to put the screens back in when it gets colder. You can also remove the insulation in the engine bay, all it does is hold in heat and make the engine quieter. It <BR>also keeps water in there and can cause rust.<BR>There's a small hole behind the battery that<BR>will allow more air in if you puch through or remove the insulation.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000011||TOM||05-10-2001||11:49 PM||||Jet Hot coating or any of the ceramic coating are great. BUT NEVER WRAP YOUR PIPES WITH ANY OF THE HEADER WRAP STUFF. They will destroy them.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||05-10-2001||11:52 PM||||I kinda have to do these things on the cheap, so Jet-hot coating is kinda outta the question for me at this time. How hard is it to remove the insulation with the engine and all in the car? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroLisa||05-10-2001||11:53 PM||||Fiero64- Thats the mustang scoop I've been lookin at buying for a while  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||sjp777||05-10-2001||11:56 PM||||Removing the screens under the deck vents makes a huge difference. Just drill out the rivets and they come right off<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||05-11-2001||01:00 AM||||TOM, why do you say we shouldn't wrap our pipes with header wrap?  I was considering using some of this wrap around my exhaust pipes.  I have seen it used with good results before on some open pipes as heat shielding on a motorcycle and it seemed to work as well as metal heat shields.  This was only temporary though, I'm not sure how it affects things long term.<P>I have been looking over EVERY facet of my car lately trying to figure out what stuff I can do easily to improve it.  I plan on removing the vent screens and screwing them in during the winter as Dave suggested because of the conditions I put my car through (Alabama summers and Michigan winters for my car, literally).<P>As an addition, I have been considering trying to make a sort of air dam for the engine compartment, like the front of a car uses for the radiator flow.  If I made a flap that hung down a few extra inches, this would create a higher pressure in front of the engine compartment and low pressure behind it to encourage the under car air to flow through the compartment (which would be further enhanced by adding a rearwards facing scoop on the deck lid.  Has anybody out there tried this?<P>I'm going to have to play around with some thoughts about having a fan in the rear.  It's an excellent idea, just trying to think of a cool/unique way to do it and be more efficient.  This thread definitely has me interested <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||TheFiero64||05-11-2001||01:00 PM||||Lisa, I got mine from here <A HREF="http://mpmotorsports.net/parts/scoops/79mustang.html" TARGET=_blank>http://mpmotorsports.net/parts/scoops/79mustang.html</A> <P>It was $63 with shipping.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||TheFiero64||05-11-2001||01:02 PM||||oops looks like they went up. It is now $70 with shipping||||reg||1
Z||000018||Dave Gunsul||05-11-2001||10:13 PM||||Well SeanH when removing the insulation you<BR>must use the delicate art of ripping.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>BTW Jet Hot will only cost about $150.00.<BR>The wrap would cost at least $60.00 so it's really not that much more and will improve power slightly. As I said above though, removing the insulation and drilling out the screens on the bottom of the deck lid vents<BR>will improve cooling and cost nothing.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
